          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lafayette-la-princesse-de-cleves-d85bf4a3587cc1b3\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The embedded negative cessative proposition, “je ne vous aimais plus” (“I no longer loved you”), represents the cessation of the letter-writer’s loving state as something the addressee had become convinced of. Its force is wholly truth-conditional under a reported-belief frame. It neither undertakes a commitment nor functions as an affective verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The clause is embedded under the writer’s report of the addressee’s conviction, so it does not straightforwardly avow the writer’s actual state. Whether that conviction was fully true is distinct from its strongly truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"reported\",\"description\":\"Within a written letter reproduced by the narration, the writer reports that her addressee had become convinced of the embedded proposition that she no longer loved him.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The writer deliberately made her reassurances appear forced so that the addressee would infer that her feelings had changed. The highlighted clause reports the resulting conviction and participates in a calculated strategy of concealment and revenge. Those deceptive and manipulative circumstances concern the proposition’s truth and use, not its core semantic mode; the adjacent resolution never to see him again also does not turn this embedded state-description into a performative undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“vous en étiez encore mieux persuadé que je ne vous aimais plus”\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted proposition is embedded as the content of the addressee’s conviction, strongly supporting truth-conditional force and reported status.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je tâchais de vous rassurer; mais c'était d'une manière si forcée”\",\"supports\":\"The addressee’s belief that she no longer loved him arose from deliberately unconvincing reassurance, establishing strategic context without adding performative or expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The surrounding passage says she intended to make him see that her feelings had changed while pretending to conceal the change.\",\"supports\":\"The love-state proposition is used within calculated dissimulation and revenge rather than produced as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as French cessative, negative, present/imperfect-form discourse with the temporal modifier “no longer”; the canonical context presents it inside a letter.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the core content is cessation of loving and that the proposition occurs within written, embedded discourse.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the reported proposition about a ceased emotional state. The embedding, deliberate deception, and epistolary presentation require contextual explanation but reveal no residual core force outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification targets the first highlighted embedded occurrence in “persuadé que je ne vous aimais plus,” not the nearby later assertion “je vous ai fait voir que je ne vous aimais plus.”\"}"
